  <data name="LeadDocumentDeletedRule" xml:space="preserve"><value>Shows deletion records whose file names end in one of these extensions: {0}.</value></data>
  <data name="LeadTimeOutOfOrderRule" xml:space="preserve"><value>Compares adjacent entries in one continuous section of the journal. An entry appears here when its record number (USN) is higher and its timestamp is at least 60 seconds earlier than the preceding entry’s.</value></data>
  <data name="LeadRenamedThenDeletedRule" xml:space="preserve"><value>Finds a file that was renamed twice and then deleted. Each rename must have separate old-name and new-name records with the same timestamp. The names and parent folders must link from the first rename through the deletion within 512 records in one continuous section of the journal. Any basic information change recorded in between is shown as evidence. The journal records the change reason without identifying which timestamp or file attribute changed.</value></data>
  <data name="LeadSDeleteNameRule" xml:space="preserve"><value>Matches SDelete executable names. It also shows creation and data changes for .pf names such as SDELETE.EXE-A1B2C3D4.pf, where the executable name is followed by eight hexadecimal digits.&#10;&#10;Windows can create or update Prefetch files when a program runs. Check the .pf contents or other evidence to verify whether and when the program ran.</value></data>
